---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PageHero from "../components/PageHero.astro";
import PageIntro from "../components/PageIntro.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import Figure from "../components/Figure.astro";
import Diagram from "../components/Diagram.astro";
import { PAGE_HERO_IMAGES } from "../consts";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Radiology Fundamentals | Radiology & medicolegal law"
  description="A working radiology reference: what each modality answers and where it is limited, the RCR standards for interpretation and reporting, radiation dose and safety duties, quality assurance, and how imaging should be stored, transferred and viewed."
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="Radiology library"
    title="Radiology Fundamentals"
    description="The clinical half of this resource: what each investigation can reasonably answer, the professional standards that govern reporting, the statutory duties attached to dose and data, and how images should be handled."
    heroImage={PAGE_HERO_IMAGES["/radiology-fundamentals"] || undefined}
  />

  <PageIntro
    eyebrow="Radiology library"
    paragraphs={[
      "This section covers what each imaging modality can reasonably answer, the professional standards governing the interpretation and reporting of imaging, and the statutory requirements for dose, quality assurance and image handling.",
    ]}
    contents={[
      { href: "#modalities", label: "Modalities" },
      { href: "#dose-safety", label: "Dose and safety" },
      { href: "#reporting-standards", label: "Reporting standards" },
      { href: "#handling-images", label: "Handling images" },
      { href: "#legal-questions", label: "Legal questions" },
      { href: "#quality-assurance", label: "Quality assurance" },
      { href: "#primary-sources", label: "Primary sources" },
    ]}
    audience="Providing a clear, practical overview of day-to-day radiological principles for non-specialist readers, trainees, and experienced practitioners seeking a concise reference."
    reviewed="September 2026"
  />

  <!-- Modalities -->
  <section class="band">
    <div class="content-section">
      <Section
        id="modalities"
        index="01"
        eyebrow="Modalities"
        title="Common radiology examinations"
        lead="Each modality has its own strengths, weaknesses and clinical risks. The following sections describe what each modality can reasonably answer, where it is limited, and the practical safety considerations."
      >
        <div class="entry-list mt-0">
          <!-- 1: X-ray -->
          <div class="entry">
            <figure class="figure mt-0">
              <div class="figure-frame">
                <div class="relative aspect-[3/1] overflow-hidden">
                  <img
                    src="/images/normal-chest-radiograph-female-5.png"
                    alt=""
                    class="h-full w-full object-cover"
                  />
                </div>
              </div>
            </figure>
            <h3 class="entry-title">Radiography (X-ray)</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm font-semibold text-slate-700">
              Excellent for rapid triage and diagnosis
            </p>
            <p class="para-sm pl-3">
              The classic radiology examination, the plain radiograph, has
              historically been the mainstay of most radiology departments, but
              in recent years lower-dose CT scanners and increasingly detailed
              MRI have gradually begun to supplant this old, reliable workhorse.
              However, plain radiographs remain extremely useful and are readily
              available in almost every healthcare setting. For some
              examinations, a plain radiograph provides all the necessary
              information in a single image, allowing a faster and easier
              diagnosis that is no less accurate. In some situations, however, a
              plain radiograph is much less accurate than CT or MRI. Despite
              their relatively low radiation dose, plain radiographs will remain
              a vital part of any radiology department for many years to come.
            </p>
            <div class="mt-4 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
              <div class="pl-4">
                <h4
                  class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-slate-500"
                >
                  Common indications
                </h4>
                <ul class="mt-2 space-y-1 text-sm text-slate-600">
                  <li class="check-row">
                    <span
                      class="mt-1.5 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-slate-500"
                    ></span><span>Suspected fracture or dislocation</span>
                  </li>
                  <li class="check-row">
                    <span
                      class="mt-1.5 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-slate-500"
                    ></span><span
                      >Chest infection, consolidation or pleural effusion</span
                    >
                  </li>
                  <li class="check-row">
                    <span
                      class="mt-1.5 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-slate-500"
                    ></span><span
                      >Bowel obstruction or free intraperitoneal gas</span
                    >
                  </li>
                  <li class="check-row">
                    <span
                      class="mt-1.5 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-slate-500"
                    ></span><span>Foreign-body localisation</span>
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </div>
              <div class="pl-4">
                <h4
                  class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-slate-500"
                >
                  Limitations
                </h4>
                <ul class="mt-2 space-y-1 text-sm text-slate-600">
                  <li class="check-row">
                    <span
                      class="mt-1.5 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-slate-400"
                    ></span><span
                      >Superimposed structures can mask or mimic pathology</span
                    >
                  </li>
                  <li class="check-row">
                    <span
                      class="mt-1.5 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-slate-400"
                    ></span><span
                      >Low soft-tissue contrast compared with CT or MRI</span
                    >
                  </li>
                  <li class="check-row">
                    <span
                      class="mt-1.5 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-slate-400"
                    ></span><span
                      >Projection geometry can conceal a fracture line</span
                    >
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="mt-4 rounded-l-md border-l-[3px] border-slate-300 p-4">
              <p
                class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-slate-600"
              >
                Safety and practical notes
              </p>
              <p class="mt-1.5 pl-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-700">
                Low-dose examination. Pregnancy status should be checked where
                the abdomen or pelvis is in the primary beam. Gonad shielding is
                no longer indicated under modern collimation.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- 2: CT -->
          <div class="entry">
            <figure class="figure mt-0">
              <div class="figure-frame">
                <div class="relative aspect-[3/1] overflow-hidden">
                  <img
                    src="/images/head_ct_images.jpg"
                    alt=""
                    class="h-full w-full object-cover"
                  />
                </div>
              </div>
            </figure>
            <h3 class="entry-title">Computed tomography (CT)</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm font-semibold text-slate-700">
              Excellent for most acute and cross-sectional questions
            </p>
            <p class="para-sm pl-3">
              Computed tomography is the modern workhorse of acute and
              cross-sectional imaging, with high spatial resolution, short
              acquisition times and near-universal availability in secondary
              care. Multi-detector scanners can image the whole chest and
              abdomen in seconds, which is why CT is first line for acute
              presentations, trauma, most suspected malignancies and vascular
              disease. Its main weakness is that it uses ionising radiation at
              doses higher than those of plain radiography, and it often
              requires iodinated contrast (an iodine-based agent injected to
              make structures easier to see), which carries risks of allergy and
              renal injury. Other technical issues, such as beam-hardening and
              metal artefact, can also obscure detail -- an issue less prevalent
              with plain radiographs. Regardless, every exposure must be
              justified under IR(ME)R.
            </p>
            <div class="mt-4 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
              <div class="pl-4">
                <h4
                  class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-slate-500"
                >
                  Common indications
                </h4>
                <ul class="mt-2 space-y-1 text-sm text-slate-600">
                  <li class="check-row">
                    <span
                      class="mt-1.5 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-slate-500"
                    ></span><span
                      >Acute head injury, stroke or intracranial haemorrhage</span
                    >
                  </li>
                  <li class="check-row">
                    <span
                      class="mt-1.5 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-slate-500"
                    ></span><span
                      >Acute abdomen, trauma and suspected perforation</span
                    >
                  </li>
                  <li class="check-row">
                    <span
                      class="mt-1.5 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-slate-500"
                    ></span><span>Pulmonary embolism and aortic dissection</span
                    >
                  </li>
                  <li class="check-row">
                    <span
                      class="mt-1.5 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-slate-500"
                    ></span><span>Staging and follow-up of malignancy</span>
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </div>
              <div class="pl-4">
                <h4
                  class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-slate-500"
                >
                  Limitations
                </h4>
                <ul class="mt-2 space-y-1 text-sm text-slate-600">
                  <li class="check-row">
                    <span
                      class="mt-1.5 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-slate-400"
                    ></span><span
                      >Ionising radiation dose -- justification and optimisation
                      apply</span
                    >
                  </li>
                  <li class="check-row">
                    <span
                      class="mt-1.5 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-slate-400"
                    ></span><span
                      >Iodinated contrast risks (allergy, renal function,
                      thyroid)</span
                    >
                  </li>
                  <li class="check-row">
                    <span
                      class="mt-1.5 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-slate-400"
                    ></span><span
                      >Beam-hardening and metal artefact can obscure detail</span
                    >
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="mt-4 rounded-l-md border-l-[3px] border-slate-300 p-4">
              <p
                class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-slate-600"
              >
                Safety and practical notes
              </p>
              <p class="mt-1.5 pl-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-700">
                Justification is mandatory under IR(ME)R. Contrast-enhanced
                studies require recent renal function and allergy history. Dose
                is monitored against diagnostic reference levels.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- 3: MRI -->
          <div class="entry">
            <figure class="figure mt-0">
              <div class="figure-frame">
                <div class="relative aspect-[3/1] overflow-hidden">
                  <img
                    src="/images/pexels-cottonbro-5723878.jpg"
                    alt=""
                    class="h-full w-full object-cover"
                  />
                </div>
              </div>
            </figure>
            <h3 class="entry-title">Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI)</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm font-semibold text-slate-700">
              Excellent for soft tissues
            </p>
            <p class="para-sm pl-3">
              MRI delivers high soft-tissue contrast without ionising radiation
              and is the modality of choice for soft-tissue, marrow, joint and
              neurological questions. Multiplanar acquisition and a range of
              contrast weightings make it central to musculoskeletal,
              neurological, hepatobiliary and pelvic imaging, and it will
              demonstrate pathology that CT resolves poorly or not at all. Its
              constraints are physical rather than dosimetric. The static field
              is permanently energised, so implant, device and foreign-body
              screening is mandatory before entry to the controlled area, and
              ferromagnetic projectile injury remains the principal safety
              hazard. Acquisition times are long, motion degrades the study, and
              a proportion of patients cannot tolerate the examination.
              Gadolinium (an intravenous contrast agent) requires a documented
              renal function assessment, and there are ongoing concerns about
              tissue deposition in the brain. MRI capacity and cost continue to
              limit access compared with CT. Its principal advantage is that it
              carries no ionising radiation and therefore no IR(ME)R
              justification requirement, but the request must still identify a
              clinical question that MRI answers better than a faster or
              lower-cost alternative.
            </p>
            <div class="mt-4 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
              <div class="pl-4">
                <h4
                  class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-slate-500"
                >
                  Common indications
                </h4>
                <ul class="mt-2 space-y-1 text-sm text-slate-600">
                  <li class="check-row">
                    <span
                      class="mt-1.5 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-slate-500"
                    ></span><span>Joints and soft-tissue injury</span>
                  </li>
                  <li class="check-row">
                    <span
                      class="mt-1.5 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-slate-500"
                    ></span><span
                      >Brain, spinal cord and peripheral nerve lesions</span
                    >
                  </li>
                  <li class="check-row">
                    <span
                      class="mt-1.5 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-slate-500"
                    ></span><span>Liver, biliary and pelvic pathology</span>
                  </li>
                  <li class="check-row">
                    <span
                      class="mt-1.5 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-slate-500"
                    ></span><span
                      >Bone marrow and musculoskeletal infection or tumour</span
                    >
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </div>
              <div class="pl-4">
                <h4
                  class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-slate-500"
                >
                  Limitations
                </h4>
                <ul class="mt-2 space-y-1 text-sm text-slate-600">
                  <li class="check-row">
                    <span
                      class="mt-1.5 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-slate-400"
                    ></span><span>Implant, device and foreign-body safety</span>
                  </li>
                  <li class="check-row">
                    <span
                      class="mt-1.5 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-slate-400"
                    ></span><span
                      >Long acquisition times, so motion degrades the study</span
                    >
                  </li>
                  <li class="check-row">
                    <span
                      class="mt-1.5 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-slate-400"
                    ></span><span>Gadolinium contrast</span>
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="mt-4 rounded-l-md border-l-[3px] border-slate-300 p-4">
              <p
                class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-slate-600"
              >
                Safety and practical notes
              </p>
              <p class="mt-1.5 pl-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-700">
                No ionising radiation, but the static magnetic field is always
                on. Implant and device status must be checked before entry to
                the controlled area. Gadolinium is avoided in significant renal
                impairment unless clinically essential.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- 4: Ultrasound -->
          <div class="entry">
            <figure class="figure mt-0">
              <div class="figure-frame">
                <div class="relative aspect-[3/1] overflow-hidden">
                  <img
                    src="/images/posterior-acoustic-shadowing-and-enhancement.jpeg"
                    alt=""
                    class="h-full w-full object-cover"
                  />
                </div>
              </div>
            </figure>
            <h3 class="entry-title">Ultrasound</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm font-semibold text-slate-700">
              Excellent for superficial structures and blood flow
            </p>
            <p class="para-sm pl-3">
              Ultrasound provides real-time imaging without ionising radiation.
              It is a dynamic examination rather than a set of static images, so
              it can assess flow and superficial structures that projection and
              cross-sectional imaging demonstrate poorly. It is widely used in
              abdominal, obstetric, musculoskeletal and soft-tissue imaging, and
              it is the guidance method for many interventional procedures. Its
              principal weakness is operator dependence: image quality and
              interpretation vary with the practitioner, and the examination is
              the least reproducible of the modalities. Penetration is limited,
              and bowel gas obscures deeper structures. Ultrasound uses no
              ionising radiation, so no IR(ME)R justification requirement
              arises.
            </p>
            <div class="mt-4 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
              <div class="pl-4">
                <h4
                  class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-slate-500"
                >
                  Common indications
                </h4>
                <ul class="mt-2 space-y-1 text-sm text-slate-600">
                  <li class="check-row">
                    <span
                      class="mt-1.5 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-slate-500"
                    ></span><span
                      >Abdominal and pelvic pain, including gallstones and renal
                      obstruction</span
                    >
                  </li>
                  <li class="check-row">
                    <span
                      class="mt-1.5 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-slate-500"
                    ></span><span
                      >Obstetric assessment and foetal wellbeing</span
                    >
                  </li>
                  <li class="check-row">
                    <span
                      class="mt-1.5 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-slate-500"
                    ></span><span
                      >Vascular stenosis, thrombosis and flow assessment</span
                    >
                  </li>
                  <li class="check-row">
                    <span
                      class="mt-1.5 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-slate-500"
                    ></span><span
                      >Image guidance for biopsy, drainage and vascular access</span
                    >
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </div>
              <div class="pl-4">
                <h4
                  class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-slate-500"
                >
                  Limitations
                </h4>
                <ul class="mt-2 space-y-1 text-sm text-slate-600">
                  <li class="check-row">
                    <span
                      class="mt-1.5 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-slate-400"
                    ></span><span
                      >Operator-dependent and the least reproducible modality</span
                    >
                  </li>
                  <li class="check-row">
                    <span
                      class="mt-1.5 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-slate-400"
                    ></span><span
                      >Limited penetration; bowel gas obscures deeper structures</span
                    >
                  </li>
                  <li class="check-row">
                    <span
                      class="mt-1.5 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-slate-400"
                    ></span><span
                      >No whole-body overview compared with CT or MRI</span
                    >
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="mt-4 rounded-l-md border-l-[3px] border-slate-300 p-4">
              <p
                class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-slate-600"
              >
                Safety and practical notes
              </p>
              <p class="mt-1.5 pl-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-700">
                No ionising radiation. Output parameters are kept as low as
                reasonably practicable. Endocavitary and interventional probes
                require appropriate decontamination between patients.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <Figure
          src="/images/reporting_radiographer.jpg"
          alt="A radiographer reviewing a series of CT images on a monitor"
          label="Figure 01"
          caption="A radiographer checks a series of CT images on a monitor"
          subtitle="No modality is better than the others: each has its own clinical use, with its own strengths and limitations."
          ratio="wide"
        />
      </Section>

      <!-- Dose and safety -->
      <Section
        id="dose-safety"
        index="02"
        eyebrow="Dose & safety"
        title="Information on Dose, justification and statutory duties"
        lead="Ionising radiation is a powerful diagnostic tool, but it carries a risk of harm. The statutory duties of justification and optimisation apply to every exposure, and the law requires that the dose be kept as low as reasonably practicable for the diagnostic purpose."
      >
        <Figure
          label="Figure 02"
          caption="How the modalities compare on dose"
          subtitle="Linear scale 0–20 mSv. MRI and ultrasound involve no ionising radiation."
        >
          <Diagram name="dose-scale" />
        </Figure>

        {/* Reference table to accompany the diagram */}
        <div class="mt-6 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2">
          <div class="rounded border border-slate-200 bg-white p-4">
            <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-slate-900">
              UK average annual effective dose (all sources)
            </p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xl font-bold text-slate-800">2.6 mSv</p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-slate-500">
              About half from radon; wide regional variation.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded border border-slate-200 bg-white p-4">
            <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-slate-900">
              Average annual dose in Cornwall
            </p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xl font-bold text-slate-800">7.8 mSv</p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-slate-500">
              Elevated radon; the highest regional average in the UK.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded border border-slate-200 bg-white p-4">
            <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-slate-900">
              Public dose limit (man-made sources, excluding medical)
            </p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xl font-bold text-slate-800">1 mSv/year</p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-slate-500">
              Statutory limit under the Basic Safety Standards.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded border border-slate-200 bg-white p-4">
            <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-slate-900">
              Chest radiograph (for comparison)
            </p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xl font-bold text-slate-800">≈0.02 mSv</p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-slate-500">
              A single projection; dose varies with technique.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded border border-slate-200 bg-white p-4">
            <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-slate-900">
              Occupational dose limit (effective dose) i.e. radiation workers
            </p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xl font-bold text-slate-800">20 mSv/year</p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-slate-500">
              Averaged over any calendar year; 100 mSv over five years under
              IRR17.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="entry-list mt-6">
          <div class="entry">
            <h3 class="entry-title">
              Justification and optimisation keywords explained
            </h3>
            <ul class="list pl-4">
              <li>
                <strong>Justification:</strong> The benefit of the exposure to the
                patient must outweigh any potential harm. The referrer must provide
                sufficient clinical information to allow the practitioner to justify
                the examination and authorise the exposure.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Optimisation:</strong> Radiation doses are kept as low as
                reasonably practicable (ALARP) while achieving the required diagnostic
                purpose. The 2024 amendments to IR(ME)R strengthened duties to have
                regard for diagnostic reference levels and to audit practice.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Diagnostic reference levels (DRLs):</strong> National values
                derived from wide-scale surveys, typically set at the third quartile
                of the dose distribution for standard practice. Local DRLs should
                sit below national values, and departments consistently exceeding
                them must review their practice and take corrective action.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Protocol choice:</strong> Ensuring the chosen study is capable
                of answering the specific clinical question asked. A technically adequate
                scan that fails to address the clinical query does not constitute
                optimised care.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="entry">
            <h3 class="entry-title">Who are the duty holders under IR(ME)R?</h3>
            <ul class="list pl-4">
              <li>
                <strong>Employer:</strong> Establishes the organisational framework,
                including written procedures, protocols, training, staff entitlements,
                and compliance.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Referrer:</strong> Provides sufficient and accurate clinical
                information to enable the justification of an exposure.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Practitioner:</strong> Justifies and authorises the individual
                exposure by weighing clinical benefits against potential risks.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Operator:</strong> Carries out the practical aspects of the
                procedure and ensures optimisation in practice.
              </li>
            </ul>
            <div class="mt-4 rounded-l-md border-l-[3px] border-slate-300 p-4">
              <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-700 pl-3">
                The roles are distinct and may be held by different people for
                the same exposure. A claim can attach to any one of them
                independently.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="entry">
            <h3 class="entry-title">What needs recording and reporting</h3>
            <ul class="list pl-4">
              <li>
                Significant accidental or unintended exposures are reportable
                incidents and must be investigated, with the findings recorded.
              </li>
              <li>
                Occupational exposure is controlled through designated areas,
                monitoring and dose limits. Under IRR17 a controlled area is
                required where a person is likely to receive more than 6 mSv per
                year, and a supervised area above 1 mSv per year.
              </li>
              <li>
                Dose limits under IRR17 are 20 mSv per year (100 mSv over five
                years), 150 mSv for the lens of the eye and 500 mSv for the
                extremities.
              </li>
              <li>
                Equipment must be commissioned, tested and withdrawn from use
                when faulty.
              </li>
              <li>
                QA records and dose records are evidence. They are routinely
                sought years after the event, and their absence is itself a
                finding.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
          <a href="/radiology-legislation" class="btn btn-ghost"
            >IR(ME)R and IRR17 in full
          </a>
        </div>
      </Section>

      <Figure
        src="/images/pexels-konrads-photo-32251894.jpg"
        alt="Radiology image for reporting standards"
        label="Figure 03"
        caption="The importance of reporting standards"
        ratio="wide"
      />

      <!-- Reporting standards -->
      <Section
        id="reporting-standards"
        index="03"
        eyebrow="Standards"
        title="Interpretation and reporting: the professional benchmark"
        lead="The Royal College of Radiologists' Standards for interpretation and reporting of imaging investigations (third edition, October 2025) define what a defensible report looks like."
      >
        <div class="entry-list mt-0">
          <div class="entry">
            <h3 class="entry-title">Report content</h3>
            <p class="para-sm pl-3">
              A report must answer the clinical question asked, describe the
              findings, state the limitations of the study and give a clear
              conclusion. A structured format is expected where one has been
              agreed for that investigation. The conclusion should provide a
              radiological diagnosis or a reasoned differential, acknowledge
              limitations and make recommendations aligned with recognised
              guidelines.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="entry">
            <h3 class="entry-title">Named author and accountability</h3>
            <p class="para-sm pl-3">
              Every report must be attributable to a named reporting author,
              including where reporting is remote or assisted by software. The
              author's name, position and profession should be identifiable. The
              content must not imply a level of scrutiny that has not in fact
              been applied.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="entry">
            <h3 class="entry-title">Remote and offsite reporting</h3>
            <p class="para-sm pl-3">
              Remote reporting is acceptable only where the reporter has the
              images, the prior studies, the clinical information and the means
              to communicate an urgent finding. The same expectations of
              quality, turnaround and escalation apply as for onsite work. The
              RCR standards for teleradiology set out the governance,
              data-transfer and indemnity requirements.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="entry">
            <h3 class="entry-title">
              AI-assisted and speech-recognition output
            </h3>
            <p class="para-sm pl-3">
              Where software generates or transcribes content, the named human
              author remains responsible for its accuracy. Every automated
              contribution must be reviewed and approved before the report is
              released. The use of AI does not transfer accountability to the
              software provider.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="entry">
            <h3 class="entry-title">
              Turnaround and communication of critical findings
            </h3>
            <p class="para-sm pl-3">
              Providers must have procedures for turnaround times, communication
              of unexpected or time-critical findings, imaging classification
              for reporting priority, and acknowledgement of reports. A critical
              finding must be communicated directly and the communication
              documented, not left to the patient or referrer to discover.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="entry">
            <h3 class="entry-title">Addenda and corrections</h3>
            <p class="para-sm pl-3">
              An addendum supplements the original report; it does not replace
              it. It must be attributable, dated and linked to the original
              study. Where a discrepancy or error is identified, the addendum
              should be non-judgemental and should trigger the local discrepancy
              and candour processes.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-6 rounded-l-md border-l-[3px] border-slate-600 p-4">
          <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-slate-900 pl-3">
            The standards apply to all who interpret and report imaging,
            regardless of professional background.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-2 pl-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-700">
            A doctor reporting within a narrower scope of practice is held to
            the same standard. Where imaged anatomy falls outside the
            interpreter's expertise, radiologist review must be agreed in
            advance. Reporting by sonographers and radiographers should occur
            within consultant-radiologist-led teams, with supervision and audit
            proportionate to the scope of practice.
          </p>
        </div>
      </Section>

      <Figure
        src="/images/pexels-charlss-gonzhu-433318654-15277956.jpg"
        alt="Radiology image for handling images"
        label="Figure 04"
        caption="Illustration for handling images section"
        ratio="wide"
