      <!-- Handling and transferring images -->
      <Section
        id="handling-images"
        index="04"
        eyebrow="Handling images"
        title="Storage, transfer and viewing"
        lead="Imaging disputes and clinical investigations frequently turn on digital provenance: what data was acquired, how metadata was handled, and who accessed the study. Rigorous image handling is an essential component of the clinical and legal record."
      >
        <div class="entry-list mt-0 space-y-8">
          <div class="entry">
            <h3 class="entry-title">Anatomy of a DICOM study</h3>
            <p class="para-sm pl-3">
              A complete DICOM dataset comprises both pixel matrix data and an
              immutable header containing metadata: patient and study
              identifiers, acquisition parameters, exposure indices, display
              presets, and slice geometry. This metadata enables searchability,
              secure transfer, and anonymisation, while allowing subsequent
              reviewers to distinguish between native acquisition features,
              windowing choices, and actual pathology. Simply obscuring
              identifiers on the visible image without stripping the DICOM
              header leaves the file fully identifiable.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="divider"></div>

          <div class="entry">
            <h3 class="entry-title">Secure transfer and anonymisation</h3>
            <p class="para-sm pl-3">
              When a study is transmitted outside the primary care pathway—such
              as for specialist second opinions or medicolegal review—it must be
              anonymised at source, transferred via secure encrypted channels,
              and audit-logged. Royal College of Radiologists (RCR)
              confidentiality standards distinguish between fully anonymised
              data (stripped of all personal identifiers, dates of birth, and
              facility tags) and pseudonymised data, which legally remains
              personal data as re-linking keys exist. All transfers must comply
              with institutional information governance protocols.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="divider"></div>

          <div class="entry">
            <h3 class="entry-title">Second opinions and audit trails</h3>
            <p class="para-sm pl-3">
              The initiation of a second opinion, the credentials of the
              reviewing clinician, and the formal advice rendered must be
              documented within the clinical record. Where a secondary
              interpretation diverges from the original report, formal
              institutional discrepancy and escalation pathways must be invoked
              to ensure patient safety and clear clinical accountability.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </Section>
    </div>
  </section>
